of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:{\\begin{aligned}x=9-3t \\\\ y=5+2t \\\\ z=-7+t\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,Π:4x+3y+5z-11=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059450/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e91084ca6463f8dfa40c9cf75a3f2dee88d00a005a7e2d68df60aa979f6909c" "basis" 1917 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059450/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-108337" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3y+5z-11 & =0 \\\\ 4(9-3t)+3(5+2t)+5(-7+t) & =11 \\\\ 36-12t+15+6t-35+5t & =11 \\\\ -t & =-5 \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=5$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=9-3(5)=-6 \\\\ y=5+2(5)=15 \\\\ z=-7+5=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is  $P(-6,15,-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059450/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e91084ca6463f8dfa40c9cf75a3f2dee88d00a005a7e2d68df60aa979f6909c" "basis" 1917 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059450/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-108337" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059450/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a0a0f737738e6614dcf54d88095202b82a3ce826a18d4819f8dc807f491c853" "basis" 1917 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear